The method has four versions, all run on the same snapshots. `overlay_v1` used one 12 m buffer. Over the snapshot years much of Berlin's A and B moved from tags on the road centreline to separately mapped ways, which lie further from the network line, so v1 measured that change in mapping practice more than the network. `overlay_v2` gives separately mapped ways a 25 m buffer. `overlay_v3` tests the minimum part length on the pieces of one tier merged across touching way-sides, so the result no longer depends on how finely OSM splits its ways. `overlay_v4` stops a match at the free end of a network line, so sidepaths of the crossing or continuing street near that end no longer count; an end is free unless another line of the same class meets it within 1 m and leaves the joint within the maximum angle. Each version's figures and the checks behind it are in `docs/review/m8-overlay.md`.

The series on the page is the change in mapped A+B km per side on each network class since {{ f.mapped_baseline | day }}, the plan's first target year, one point per snapshot [M8 spec §4.4]. The level is not comparable with the plan: it includes infrastructure that predates the plan, and mapped km are not km completed to the Mobilitätsgesetz standard [ADR 0006]. The series is sensitive to how mappers draw cycleways and to the matching method. It moves when ways are redrawn and with each method version, and pieces at junctions can be matched or dropped wrongly; the log measures both kinds of error for v4.

